<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-11-5">
  <title>Resources for 11/5</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the Completeness Axiom.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove that our construction of the real numbers as equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences of rational numbers satisfies the Completeness Axiom.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Have a good intuitive understanding of what it means for <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow c} f(x) = L</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Connect your intuitive understanding of limit to the formal definition of <em>limit</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Guess the limit of a given function.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Verify the guess of the limit of a function using the definition of limit.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          State precisely what it means for a limit not to exist.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-construct-completeness-2">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Proving the Completeness Axiom (Continued from Last Class)</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on proving the real numbers satisfy the <em>completeness axiom</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="xcbtQ2sFj_c" />

    <example>
      <title>Completeness Axiom</title>
       <p>
        Let <m>S</m> be a set of real numbers that is bounded above by a real number <m>B = [ \{ b_1,b_2,b_3, \ldots \}]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let's get familiar with how we construct the supremum of <m>S</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Use the Well-Ordering Principle to prove that there is a smallest integer <m>x_1</m> that is an upper bound of <m>S</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that <m>x_1-1</m> is not an upper bound of <m>S</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  When should we choose <m>x_2=x_1-\frac12</m> and when should be choose <m>x_2=x_1</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove <m>x_2-\frac12</m> is not an upper bound of <m>S</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  How should be choose <m>x_3</m> from among <m>x_2-\frac13</m> and <m>x_2</m>?  Prove that <m>x_3-\frac13</m> is not an upper bound of <m>S</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Do the same as above for <m>x_4</m> and <m>x_5</m> and, in general <m>x_n</m>. You will probably define <m>x_n</m> recursively.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that <m>\{ x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4, \ldots \}</m> is decreasing.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Try proving that <m>\{ x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4, \ldots \}</m> is bounded.  Hint: Let <m>s=[ \{ s_1, s_2, s_3, \ldots \} ] \in S</m>, and use the sequence <m>\{ s_n \}</m> to find a lower bound for all the <m>x_n</m>'s.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Here is a sliver of proving that every ordered field that contains <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> and satisfies the Completeness Axiom is equivalent to the real numbers we constructed with Cauchy sequences.  Let <m>\mathbb{R}</m> be <q>our</q> real numbers, and let <m>F</m> be an ordered field that contains <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> and satisfies the Completeness Axiom.  We seek to find a function <m>g: F \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> that takes each number in <m>F</m> to the number in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> that plays the same role.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Let <m>x</m> be in <m>F</m>.  Find a set of rational numbers that <m>x</m> is the supremum of.  Prove your result.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  In the set you found above, can you find a sequence of rational numbers that converges to <m>x</m>?  You will probably need to define it recursively.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove your sequence in (b) is Cauchy.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Determine what <m>g(x)</m> is based on your work in (a)--(c).
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove <m>g</m> is well defined.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-intro-limits">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Introduction to Limits</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>limits</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="4zG2_dh_6zU" />

    <example>
      <title>Introduction to Limits</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              For each of the following, guess <m>\displaystyle L = \lim_{x \rightarrow a} f(x)</m>, and for <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>,  determine inequalities involving <m>|x-a|</m> for which <m>\left| f(x) - L \right| &lt; \epsilon</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a = 2</m>, <m>f(x) = 3x+4</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a=5</m>, <m>f(x) = \frac{2x}{x+3}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a</m> arbitrary, <m>f(x) = mx+b</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a=3</m>, <m>f(x)=x^2</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the definition of limits to prove your guesses for the limits in #1 are correct. Hint: For (b) and (d), start by demanding <m>\delta \leq 1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <md> f(x) = \begin{cases} 2x+4, \amp x \leq 1 \\ -3x+5, \amp x &gt; 1 \end{cases}</md> and assume <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow 1} f(x) = L</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\delta &gt; 0</m>, prove that there is some <m>0 \le x &lt; 1</m> such that <m>0 &lt; |x-1| &lt; \delta</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Use the above with <m>\epsilon=1</m> to prove that <m>L &gt; 3</m>.  Hint: Use <m>|f(x)-L| &lt; \epsilon</m> to solve for <m>L</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\delta &gt; 0</m>, prove that there is some <m>1 &lt; x \le 2</m> such that <m>0 &lt; |x-1| &lt; \delta</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Use the above with <m>\epsilon=1</m> to prove that <m>L &lt; 3</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What does this say about <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow 1}  f(x)</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              State precisely what it means for <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow a} f(x)</m> not to exist.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

